{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2530 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "8e37f8b6ddb6d7fd81bea19641786b59e766832f83232a43a8b92ae108947441" :edb/db-before-t 2529 :edb/db-after-t 2530 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535842 50 #inst "2026-10-09T08:42:25.783Z" 13194139535842 true] [17592186105812 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369675 13194139535842 true] [17592186105812 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369682 13194139535842 true] [17592186105813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369661 13194139535842 true] [17592186105813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369668 13194139535842 true] [17592186105986 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535842 true] [17592186105986 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/assets/365c6e6ebc861b9d8276773d5afe39b971096f0de6f07fced2c5a2ea9e035554.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}3x\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x-y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$ in the first quadrant, as shown above." 13194139535842 true] [17592186105986 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1264/Source/Images/q-161146.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n$∬_(S)3xdS$,\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z = x - y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$ in the first quadrant, as shown above." 13194139535842 false] [17592186105986 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}3x\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,3x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,3x\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\cdot \\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}3{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [{r}^{3}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369661 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369662 13194139535842 true] [17592186369661 1089 "q-161291" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369661 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535842 true] [17592186369661 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/assets/9795c04bf6d4bfa407a7b9176cd5c4b97b3d89bfcc6fff17d1d78190047b3a54.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}6y\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ and whose domain is the upper half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." 13194139535842 true] [17592186369661 1113 #uuid "9649fbd0-1b6c-528b-8da4-a1be1daa517f" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369661 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}6y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6y\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6y\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,6y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,6y\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\cdot \\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}(1+1) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369662 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369663 13194139535842 true] [17592186369662 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369664 13194139535842 true] [17592186369662 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369665 13194139535842 true] [17592186369662 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369666 13194139535842 true] [17592186369662 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369667 13194139535842 true] [17592186369662 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535842 true] [17592186369662 1041 "selection" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369662 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369667 13194139535842 true] [17592186369662 1110 #uuid "f304aab7-8cf3-5f8d-9a8a-5c49bf869a77" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369663 1034 "2\\sqrt{3}" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369663 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535842 true] [17592186369663 1111 #uuid "cbb35f73-000b-5dd0-9af7-8405ff0c229c" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369664 1034 "\\sqrt{3}" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369664 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535842 true] [17592186369664 1111 #uuid "71ab6b1f-7537-56c7-a91d-cc35ada3305d" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369665 1034 "5\\sqrt{3}" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369665 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535842 true] [17592186369665 1111 #uuid "c30a68c3-d552-5ff5-af08-b6265e7505ae" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369666 1034 "3\\sqrt{3}" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369666 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535842 true] [17592186369666 1111 #uuid "50ddf406-d08f-5e58-8cb5-9875a83a3660" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369667 1034 "4\\sqrt{3}" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369667 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535842 true] [17592186369667 1111 #uuid "cef52014-d237-5950-a5cd-c78abe0cfbe8" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369668 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369669 13194139535842 true] [17592186369668 1089 "q-161311" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369668 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535842 true] [17592186369668 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/assets/5ba2dbc746b9fe8b36202d31bbeb8193ffb9e747d6ed4c2e2f0f994499f88e8e.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=-x+\\sqrt{2}y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O$ in the second quadrant, as shown above." 13194139535842 true] [17592186369668 1113 #uuid "0f99d977-1723-5815-b4ef-c6c2935d34e1" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369668 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x+\\sqrt{2}y)=-1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x+\\sqrt{2}y)=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{ }\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\,]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}(-1)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369669 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369670 13194139535842 true] [17592186369669 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369671 13194139535842 true] [17592186369669 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369672 13194139535842 true] [17592186369669 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369673 13194139535842 true] [17592186369669 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369674 13194139535842 true] [17592186369669 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535842 true] [17592186369669 1041 "selection" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369669 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369674 13194139535842 true] [17592186369669 1110 #uuid "fc1f8a23-5e32-5981-abd8-f80e77b735c0" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369670 1034 "3" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369670 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535842 true] [17592186369670 1111 #uuid "88283c52-40b6-56b7-af29-e18d67ef32e4" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369671 1034 "2" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369671 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535842 true] [17592186369671 1111 #uuid "63151eb9-bf57-55b5-8fdc-afac770aad35" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369672 1034 "\\frac{1}{6}" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369672 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535842 true] [17592186369672 1111 #uuid "463544d0-14db-5630-bc6d-9a6a8fc9d023" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369673 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369673 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535842 true] [17592186369673 1111 #uuid "ef52d9b5-c02a-5137-9a54-c47613c02fe7" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369674 1034 "-6" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369674 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535842 true] [17592186369674 1111 #uuid "f651c958-8371-533f-92a3-b2fd0adf1911" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369675 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369676 13194139535842 true] [17592186369675 1089 "q-161341" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369675 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535842 true] [17592186369675 1100 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}4z\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369675 1113 #uuid "47133f58-913f-5380-a818-45651da6032d" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369675 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}4z\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,4(x+y)\\,\\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4(x+y)\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[4xy+2{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{[2{x}^{2}+2x]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535842 true] [17592186369676 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369677 13194139535842 true] [17592186369676 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369678 13194139535842 true] [17592186369676 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369679 13194139535842 true] [17592186369676 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369680 13194139535842 true] [17592186369676 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369681 13194139535842 true] [17592186369676